WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.410
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Drug
research and development

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.960
is more challenging than ever,
requiring massive amounts

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.875
of data and complex analysis.

00:00:05.875 --> 00:00:08.250
Join us as we talk to Recursion
Pharmaceuticals about how

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.792
they're using machine learning
to speed up the drug discovery

00:00:10.792 --> 00:00:12.530
process on this
episode of Stack Chat.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:18.380
Scott, thanks so much
for joining us today.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:19.120
Can you tell us
a little bit more

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:20.908
about what Recursion
Pharmaceuticals does?

00:00:20.908 --> 00:00:21.700
SCOTT NIELSEN: Yes.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:28.020
Recursion is-- we're trying
to bring new drugs to market.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.910
And in order to do that,
we have to find new drugs,

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:31.750
discover new drugs.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:35.230
And we're trying to turn that
problem into a machine learning

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:35.800
problem.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.950
What that means and
how we do that is,

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:44.260
we are creating a large data
set consisting primarily

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:48.580
of cell microscopy images.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:51.980
And so that means we take a lot
of pictures of a lot of cells

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:53.230
in a lot of different states--

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:56.380
so healthy cells,
sick cells, sick cells

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:59.200
combined with drugs, healthy
cells combined with drugs.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.200
And try to learn
more information

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.432
about how those images change
under different circumstances.

00:01:05.432 --> 00:01:07.390
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So what
does the architecture

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:10.180
look like that does the eventual
machine learning on these?

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.640
SCOTT NIELSEN: Currently,
we're taking about 60 terabytes

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:14.800
of images a week.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.190
And so at the
beginning, that can

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:21.520
take across seven
different microscopes

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:26.470
about five days at times
to take that many images.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.080
And so we're constantly--
as soon as those images are

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.480
available, we're
uploading them into GCS,

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:34.060
and sending off events
that those images are

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.810
available for processing.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:38.680
And then we have a couple of
different Kubernetes clusters

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:39.687
running on GKE.

00:01:39.687 --> 00:01:41.270
All our software is
deployed in there,

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.090
and we have a number
of applications

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:44.740
that are reacting
to these events,

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:46.540
aggregating them at
different levels,

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:51.100
and then scheduling
processing in our kind of jobs

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.340
Kubernetes cluster to
do different computation

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.750
on the images or the results
of a previous processing,

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:03.100
until we boil those down
into more refined features

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.820
through this feature
extraction process.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:08.345
And then those features are
used, and analysis is done.

00:02:08.345 --> 00:02:10.470
Runs through-- depending
on the type of experiment,

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:11.887
it runs through
different analyses

00:02:11.887 --> 00:02:14.320
where all these results,
all these metrics,

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.530
quality metrics on the images,
and a lot of different things

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:20.680
are stored in a combination
of Cloud SQL and BigQuery.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:25.960
And then all that data is used
to make decisions and design

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.620
our next round of experiments.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.530
And so that's kind
of the process

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:31.613
that we go through at
Recursion on a weekly basis.

00:02:31.613 --> 00:02:33.988
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So it sounds
like you did a lot of data

00:02:33.988 --> 00:02:36.070
processing directly in
Kubernetes with Kubernetes

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:38.590
jobs, rather than using
a traditional big data

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:40.810
solution like Hadoop or Spark.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:43.570
What made you kind of choose
to write your own Kubernetes?

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:45.737
SCOTT NIELSEN: I think there
is a number of factors.

00:02:45.737 --> 00:02:49.840
So the two primary factors that
pushed us in that direction

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.970
was that we have all
these images coming off

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:57.220
the microscopes constantly.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:01.000
And so we have this kind of
streaming flow of images.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.570
And in order to wait for
the right batches that

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:07.690
are the most important, it
can take sometimes hours.

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:10.480
But we want certain feedback
on a lot of those images

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.820
much more quickly.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:16.400
And so we wanted a
streaming solution

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.300
so we could get faster feedback
on a lot of these quality

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.580
metrics and stop problems
and save ourselves

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:25.980
money from taking pictures
that aren't valuable.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.360
The second part of that
was we had a number

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:30.610
of existing services
that weren't written

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:33.220
in languages or in ways
that integrate nicely

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:34.700
into some of those tools.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:39.160
And so some of those were
Cloud Functions or Lambda jobs

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:42.880
and other things that we
wanted to be able to not

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:46.420
have to rewrite all of that
in order to just orchestrate

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:47.290
this pipeline.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:47.660
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Awesome.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.300
Well, thanks so much for
coming in and telling us

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.920
a little bit about how you
built this entire solution,

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:54.820
taking a real big
problem, big data problem,

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:56.640
and turning it
into real insights.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:58.717
SCOTT NIELSEN: Yeah, thank you.

00:03:58.717 --> 00:04:00.550
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Check
out this quick guide

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:02.350
to try running Kubernetes
jobs of your own.

00:04:05.042 --> 00:04:07.000
Thanks for watching, and
make sure to subscribe

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.400
for more great Google
Cloud Platform content.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.670
We'll see you next
time on Stack Chat.

